 The project's agent instructions file (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, CONVENTIONS.md, …) often names the canonical paths and invariants — treat those as the project-specific seam map. Don't stop at this list — it covers the typical spots, not all of them. Find the seams specific to your feature.
 
+**Then find what already expects your code.** The list above is built from what you already know to look for; the seams that bite are the ones you don't. The behavior you're adding is often anticipated elsewhere — the rest of the system was built around it. Search the **whole repository** (code, templates, docs — not only the module you're editing) for the feature's key nouns and for the names of every field, setting, flag, log type, signal and template your code will touch. Every hit that describes or anticipates the behavior is an inbound seam:
+
+- an **extension point** whose docs say it is called when your code runs (a provider or plugin method, a signal or hook that nothing sends yet) — your code must call it and respect its answer;
+- a **promise** about the behavior: a setting's help text, a flag or field that nothing writes yet, a declared log/audit type, a UI or timeline that shows when the thing happens — your code must keep it;
+- the **closest sibling** doing the same kind of work (a job of the same kind, the reverse operation, the same action on another entity) — its filters, guards and edge-case rules are the house rules.
+
+Add each to the seam table like any other seam.
